# CAT3b: If a mechanical ventilator is currently reporting anything other
# than its normal operating state, and the patient's pulmonary ventilation
# is currently reduced, they collectively convey a ventilation failure.
#
# Two conditions of genuinely different KINDS, not just two different
# individuals (contrast cat3a_cardiorespiratory_arrest.rq, where both sides
# are Process-anchored impliesClinicalEvent tags). The device side is a raw
# technical fact — matched directly, the same way cat1a/cat2a match
# hasQualityState/hasCategory without a clinical-event detour: there is no
# physiological state to interpret on a device fault, so impliesClinicalEvent
# (domain mda:PhysiologicalProcess) does not apply to it. The physiological
# side reuses the ReducedPulmonaryFunction tag already entailed by
# clinicalEvents.ttl's ReducedPulmonaryFunctionCondition — see that file's
# header for why the two sides cannot be folded into one declarative axiom
# (would require an unverified instance-level administers/targetsProcess
# propagation from Device through to Process).
#
# mda:hasOperationState, NOT mda:hasDeviceOperationState, deliberately, even
# though the archetype only ever asserts the latter directly. Confirmed by
# direct trace (op_knowledge.py's Timeline), not assumed: while an alarm is
# ACTIVE, both the raw hasDeviceOperationState triple AND its RDFS/OWL-RL-
# promoted mda:hasOperationState superproperty triple are present in the
# query graph (entailed_situation's own reasoning pass produces the
# promotion). But mda:persistsPostAlarm is tagged on mda:hasOperationState
# ONLY (ontology.ttl's "Post-alarm persistence classification" section) —
# op_knowledge.py's persisting_predicates()/_extract_states() literally
# iterate that exact predicate, nothing else — so once the fault alarm
# itself ends, ONLY the promoted mda:hasOperationState survives into the
# post-alarm window (Timeline.situation_at's persistence loop merges in
# _persisted_for's output, which is hasOperationState-only by construction);
# the specific mda:hasDeviceOperationState triple is gone the instant the
# alarm itself ends, the same way mda:impliesClinicalEvent is gone the instant
# ITS alarm ends, just for a different reason (situational tagging there,
# vs. simply never being in persisting_predicates here). A rule matching
# hasDeviceOperationState specifically would therefore only ever catch the
# device fault while its OWN alarm is still active — silently unable to
# exercise the 15-minute persistence window mda:hasOperationState is
# specifically tagged persistsPostAlarm to provide, exactly the gap this
# rule needs (the ventilator fault and the reduced-pulmonary-function alarm
# are two independent alarms, not simultaneous by construction). Confirmed
# empirically: cat3e below (fault ends, second alarm arrives 10 minutes
# later, still within the 15-min window) did not fire until this was fixed.
#
# opstate != Enabled, not a specific fault value list (Disconnected,
# Malfunction, ...): deliberately broad — "anything wrong with the
# ventilator" per the concept's own definition, not a fixed enumeration
# that would need updating every time a new fault archetype surfaces.
# Enabled is the sole baseline "working normally" value (see inference.ttl's
# Sensor baseline operational-state axiom for the same baseline-value
# convention applied to hasSensorOperationState); everything else —
# Disconnected, Malfunction, Warning, Disabled — is a deviation from it.
#
# No ?alarm anchor, same reasoning as cat3a: this checks the merged graph —
# background + situational + whatever the arriving alarm just contributed —
# for genuine temporal overlap of both conditions right now, not proximity.
# Fires on every arrival for as long as both hold; mda_poc_assessment.py's
# minting step (mint_ventilation_failure) collapses repeat firings into one
# ClinicalEvent per genuine episode, deduplicated on the same
# (deviceNode, processNode) pair, the same rising-edge shape as cat3a's own
# open_pairs tracking.
#
# Real corpus archetypes (see DATA/ANNOTATION/consensus/p75_annotated.csv):
# "DATEXOHMEDACOMVENTILATOR - Patient Disconnected" (Device.OperationalState
# Disconnected) for the device side, "MEDIBUS - MV ondergrens"
# (RespirationVolume, Minute, Decreased) for the physiological side.
#
# Validated against DATA/CAT_evaluation: cat3b_pos (overlapping alarms,
# fires), cat3b_neg (fault ends, second alarm arrives 20 minutes later —
# past the 15-min window — does not fire), cat3d (same as cat3b_pos,
# arrival order swapped, confirms order does not matter), cat3e (fault
# ends, second alarm arrives 10 minutes later — inside the 15-min window —
# fires, exercising persistence specifically, unlike cat3b_pos's direct
# overlap) — see cat_rules_report.md.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX device: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/device/>
PREFIX opstate: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/operation-state/>
PREFIX clinicalEvent: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/clinical-event/>

SELECT ?deviceNode ?processNode WHERE {
  ?deviceNode a device:MechanicalVentilator ;
              mda:hasOperationState ?state .
  FILTER(?state != opstate:Enabled)

  ?processNode mda:impliesClinicalEvent clinicalEvent:ReducedPulmonaryFunction .
}
